WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.221
A moulded part shrinks as it cools. Two settings decide

2
00:00:04.321 --> 00:00:09.092
how much: hold pressure and melt temperature. Lower is better.

3
00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:13.938
This map is the truth. Nobody gets to see it. All an engineer

4
00:00:14.038 --> 00:00:18.635
can do is run the process at a setting and read one number.

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.834
Start where most people start: everything

6
00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.177
low. The part shrinks sixteen.

7
00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:29.590
One factor at a time. Hold the pressure, and try the temperature high.

8
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:32.799
Eight. Better. Keep the high temperature.

9
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:36.119
Now hold that, and try the pressure high.

10
00:00:36.216 --> 00:00:38.564
Ten. Worse. Go back.

11
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.378
Three runs, every one of them measured perfectly, and

12
00:00:42.478 --> 00:00:45.846
the procedure stops at eight. It looks finished.

13
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:51.161
Look at the one corner it never ran: high pressure, low temperature.

14
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.710
A design that runs all four corners finds it. Six.

15
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.868
Four runs instead of three, and the answer one at a time gave

16
00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:05.444
is 33 per cent worse. Noise had nothing to do with it.

17
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:10.774
The reason is the interaction. Raising the temperature cuts shrinkage by

18
00:01:10.874 --> 00:01:15.151
eight at low pressure, and adds four at high pressure. What

19
00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:19.082
one factor does depends on the other, and a walk that

20
00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:21.753
moves one at a time never sees that.

21
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.924
One factor at a time is not slow. It is wrong. Change

22
00:01:26.024 --> 00:01:30.966
the factors together, and the design sees what they do together.

